Progress Report #1 (March 27)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
General Description of Project Progress
Since the proposal was finalized, we have completed the architectural design of the system
and begun implementation of core components. The major design decisions are:
1. Connection Model: We are using a P2P host/join model over TCP sockets. One player
starts the game with --host --port <PORT> and listens for a connection. The other
player connects with --join <hostname> <PORT>. This works across different linprog
machines (e.g., linprog1 to linprog3) since they share the same internal network.
No central server is required.
2. Game Design ("Army Clash"): Each player receives a budget of 200 points to build
a custom army from six unit types, each with distinct stats and counter relationships:
- Infantry (10 pts) — basic ground unit
- Tank (30 pts) — strong vs infantry, high HP and DEF
- Artillery (35 pts) — fires first each round (priority strike), high ATK
- Navy/Destroyer (25 pts) — counters missiles with bonus DEF
- Missile Launcher (40 pts) — ignores enemy DEF entirely, but fragile
- Shield Wall (20 pts) — absorbs damage for other units, no ATK
This rock-paper-scissors counter system provides strategic depth while keeping
the design scope manageable.
3. Battle Flow: Once both players submit their armies, the compositions and a shared
RNG seed are exchanged over the TCP connection. The battle then runs deterministically
on both machines simultaneously — no real-time player input during combat. Each
round consists of an artillery priority phase, a main combat phase with type bonuses,
shield absorption, and cleanup of dead units. Results are printed live to both terminals.
4. Concurrency: The main thread handles the game UI (army design, battle display),
while a dedicated network thread (POSIX thread) handles send/recv operations.
A mutex and condition variable synchronize the transition between game phases.
SIGINT is caught to handle graceful disconnection.
5. Software Architecture: The codebase is organized into the following modules:
- main.cpp: Entry point, argument parsing, thread management
- network.cpp: TCP socket setup, connection handling, send/recv
- game.cpp: Army design text UI, budget tracking
- battle.cpp: Round-by-round simulation engine
- units.cpp: Unit type definitions, stats, type interaction logic
- protocol.cpp: Serialization/deserialization of game messages
- Makefile: Build system
